00:20:20.261 --> 00:20:24.500
But pay that in
the signature size.

00:20:24.501 --> 00:20:27.519
And if you try to reduce
the signature size and

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:31.572
reduce the number of ANDs, the
linear layer of LowMC becomes

00:20:31.573 --> 00:20:34.672
very expensive and
the runtimes explode, but

00:20:34.673 --> 00:20:37.790
you'll get smaller and
smaller signatures.

00:20:41.003 --> 00:20:43.910
So we've selected instances and

00:20:43.911 --> 00:20:48.589
some tradeoff between
a runtime and signature size.

00:20:55.013 --> 00:21:02.899
So when we started to implement
the signature schemes,

00:21:02.900 --> 00:21:08.604
we already got some
good runtime numbers

00:21:08.605 --> 00:21:13.471
with around 30 milliseconds for

00:21:13.472 --> 00:21:20.015
signing and verification
was a lot faster with

00:21:20.016 --> 00:21:25.082
around 17 or 16 milliseconds.

00:21:25.083 --> 00:21:26.504
But since then,

00:21:26.505 --> 00:21:31.380
we were able to optimize these
algorithms a lot more, and

00:21:31.381 --> 00:21:36.766
what we submitted now to the
NIST Post-Quantum Competition,

00:21:36.767 --> 00:21:41.543
which is a lot faster signing
and verification times and

00:21:41.544 --> 00:21:44.410
almost reduced it
by a half here.

00:21:47.432 --> 00:21:48.632
All right, so

00:21:48.633 --> 00:21:53.432
we've also compared it to
some other recent proposals,

00:21:53.433 --> 00:21:58.832
in particular, to SPHINCS, which
is one of the other signature

00:21:58.833 --> 00:22:03.342
schemes which only relies
on symmetric primitives.

00:22:03.343 --> 00:22:07.718
And we've come closer to
their signing times, but

00:22:07.719 --> 00:22:10.910
we can't beat their
verification.

00:22:10.911 --> 00:22:15.293
They also achieved
smaller signatures, but

00:22:15.294 --> 00:22:20.608
pay the price in this secret
key and public key things.

00:22:23.036 --> 00:22:27.278
And then you can look
into signature schemes,

00:22:27.279 --> 00:22:32.540
which are based on structured
hardness assumptions.

00:22:32.541 --> 00:22:37.230
And there, the picture,
there's a lot,

00:22:37.231 --> 00:22:42.312
you'll find signature
schemes which are good

00:22:42.313 --> 00:22:48.630
at one of all the data points,
but not with all of them.

00:22:48.631 --> 00:22:52.265
So you'll either have large
secret keys or public keys, but

00:22:52.266 --> 00:22:56.400
smaller signatures, or larger
signatures but smaller keys.

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:04.220
But at least in those cases,
the runtime for

00:23:04.221 --> 00:23:08.900
signing and
verifying is very good.

00:23:08.901 --> 00:23:11.735
But for these latter schemes,
for example,

00:23:11.736 --> 00:23:15.100
the signature generation
algorithms are very slow.

00:23:24.020 --> 00:23:25.828
So at the first look,

00:23:25.829 --> 00:23:31.556
our signature sizes look kind of
bad with around 129 kilobytes or

00:23:31.557 --> 00:23:36.840
200 kilobytes for
the Unruh transform version.

00:23:36.841 --> 00:23:39.503
But if you look at
the security level,

00:23:39.504 --> 00:23:43.499
which in the NIST competition
is level one, which ask for

00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:48.412
a 64-bit post-quantum signature
schemes, we can get that down to

00:23:48.413 --> 00:23:52.994
around 30 kilobytes for the
Fiat-Shamir transform version or

00:23:52.995 --> 00:23:56.516
52 kilobytes for
the Unruh transform version.

00:23:56.517 --> 00:24:00.168
And also, the signing times
go down a lot to around 2

00:24:00.169 --> 00:24:02.745
milliseconds, which isn't a lot.

00:24:09.158 --> 00:24:13.370
Right, yeah, and
some of these improvements

00:24:13.371 --> 00:24:18.014
were done by one our students,
Angela Promitzer,

00:24:18.015 --> 00:24:23.630
who worked on improving LowMC
and the LowMC implementation.

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:29.988
And so I present to you
the LowMC encryption.

00:24:29.989 --> 00:24:34.816
This looks like it's just very
less attractive, where you

00:24:34.817 --> 00:24:39.839
start with you'll add an initial
round key to the plaintext,

00:24:39.840 --> 00:24:43.896
and then you'll apply
the same round sometimes,

00:24:43.897 --> 00:24:48.146
where you first apply
an Xbox layer, which doesn't

00:24:48.147 --> 00:24:52.228
operate on the first state,
but just parts of it.

00:24:52.229 --> 00:24:54.860
Then you'll add a linear layer,

00:24:54.861 --> 00:24:58.628
where you multiply
the state with some matrix.

00:24:58.629 --> 00:25:02.166
And then you add a round
constant and a round key.

00:25:05.212 --> 00:25:08.652
Which is.

00:25:08.653 --> 00:25:09.399
Yeah.

00:25:09.400 --> 00:25:14.570
So in our case, the important
bits are the S-box.

00:25:14.571 --> 00:25:18.350
It's the only place
where we have ends.

00:25:18.351 --> 00:25:23.090
And the other important bits
are the linear layer which is

00:25:23.091 --> 00:25:26.630
composed of the matrix
multiplication of the state

00:25:26.631 --> 00:25:29.390
with the matrix and
where we compute the round key.

00:25:31.430 --> 00:25:35.043
Which, so if you reduce
the number of S-boxes,

00:25:35.044 --> 00:25:39.365
you'll increase the number of
rounds so you'll have more

00:25:39.366 --> 00:25:43.706
matrix multiplications,
which then become expensive.

00:25:47.912 --> 00:25:52.526
So, one of the problems that we
face in the signature scheme is

00:25:52.527 --> 00:25:56.873
that the input, is that the
secret key is shared on every,

00:25:56.874 --> 00:25:59.648
freshly shared on
every execution.

00:25:59.649 --> 00:26:06.060
So you can't precompute
the round keys for example.

00:26:06.061 --> 00:26:08.970
Which would be an obvious
optimization if you'd

00:26:08.971 --> 00:26:12.880
use this as a proxy for you if
you recompute the round keys and

00:26:12.881 --> 00:26:15.380
would get rid of one matrix
multiplication that you have

00:26:15.381 --> 00:26:17.240
to do in every encryption.

00:26:17.241 --> 00:26:18.970
But we can't do that here.

00:26:18.971 --> 00:26:23.708
So, we are looking into some
optimization techniques

00:26:23.709 --> 00:26:28.554
which we can perform without
involving the secret key.

00:26:32.948 --> 00:26:37.933
So this is a typical run
of one of the LowMC runs.

00:26:37.934 --> 00:26:40.872
You'll start with the s,
with the state,

00:26:40.873 --> 00:26:43.903
and then apply the S-box
on some part of it.

00:26:43.904 --> 00:26:47.646
Then you'll have
the multiplication with one of

00:26:47.647 --> 00:26:52.870
the matrixes, and the round
constant, and the round key.

00:26:52.871 --> 00:26:56.000
And so
what we're trying to do here is

00:26:58.410 --> 00:27:03.431
this part of the state that
is not affected by the S-box.

00:27:03.432 --> 00:27:08.626
So some of those,
some part of the round key and

00:27:08.627 --> 00:27:12.231
some part of the constant only.

00:27:14.361 --> 00:27:19.840
Affects this part which is
not affected by the S-box.

00:27:19.841 --> 00:27:23.990
So we want to move everything
that doesn't touch the bits

00:27:23.991 --> 00:27:31.038
interesting for the S-box
before we start all the rounds.

00:27:31.039 --> 00:27:35.910
So, we move these

00:27:35.911 --> 00:27:40.150
matrix multiplications
up as far as we can.

00:27:40.151 --> 00:27:45.140
And then at some point we
have to split them in some

00:27:45.141 --> 00:27:50.019
patent affects,
what we call nonlinear part.

00:27:50.020 --> 00:27:54.555
Which is the part
affected by the S-box and

00:27:54.556 --> 00:28:00.478
which only affects the linear
part which we can move up.

00:28:00.479 --> 00:28:05.606
And so we repeat it until
all matrix operations that

00:28:05.607 --> 00:28:11.340
only affect the linear part
at the beginning of the loop.

00:28:14.810 --> 00:28:20.016
So we can remove the matrix
multiplication of the key

00:28:20.017 --> 00:28:26.066
with the, with this, of the key
matrix with the secret key.

00:28:26.067 --> 00:28:31.054
They're from and replace it
with one matrix multiplication

00:28:31.055 --> 00:28:34.581
in the beginning with
some precomputed matrix.

00:28:39.117 --> 00:28:43.381
And we only have to perform
one matrix multiplication in

00:28:43.382 --> 00:28:46.285
the beginning using
the secret key and

00:28:46.286 --> 00:28:51.900
kind of apply that to the state
directly before the first round.

00:28:51.901 --> 00:28:56.220
So this is for the linear part,
and for the non-linear

00:28:56.221 --> 00:29:01.010
part we can do something very
similar where we obtain for

00:29:01.011 --> 00:29:04.290
each round similarly
looking matrix.

00:29:06.590 --> 00:29:11.540
This matrix is a lot smaller
than the original matrices.

00:29:11.541 --> 00:29:17.975
And we can combine all those
matrices into one larger one and

00:29:17.976 --> 00:29:22.610
multi-perform this computation
also in the beginning.

00:29:22.611 --> 00:29:28.057
And then split up the output of
that matrix multiplication into

00:29:28.058 --> 00:29:33.622
the bits that affect each lever,
each round of the encryption.

00:29:38.461 --> 00:29:45.550
So moving those matrices around
leads to quite a significant

00:29:45.551 --> 00:29:50.700
performance increase
from around 30%.

00:29:50.701 --> 00:29:51.868
Which.

00:29:51.869 --> 00:29:56.900
So the blue bar, blue lines in

00:29:56.901 --> 00:30:02.760
the original version without the
optimization and the green lines

00:30:02.761 --> 00:30:08.870
then if you used the optimized
encryption algorithm.

00:30:08.871 --> 00:30:14.600
And we can observe similar
figures on other platforms.

00:30:14.601 --> 00:30:19.559
For example, this was, it has
the same performance gains on

00:30:19.560 --> 00:30:24.777
our Right,

00:30:24.778 --> 00:30:28.748
okay, so yeah.

